The natural frequency of a helical spring is given by $$f_n = (2 \pi/L) sqrt(G/W)$$ where L is the effective length of the spring, G is the modulus of rigidity and W is the weight of the spring per unit length. 
(1) For one end fixed and one end free, the effective length of the spring L = L1 = 12d = 12 * 0.5 in = 6 in. 
$$f_n = (2 \pi/6) sqrt(11.5 × 10^6/0.285) = 28.94 cycles/sec$$

(2) For both ends fixed, the effective length of the spring L = L2 = d = 0.5 in.
$$f_n = (2 \pi/0.5) sqrt(11.5 × 10^6/0.285) = 57.88 cycles/sec$$

The operating load frequency is 120 cycles/min = 2 cycles/s. The ratio of operating frequency to fundamental frequency should not exceed 1 but must be greater than 0.2. Thus, 0.2 < f/f_n < 1 or 0.2 < 2/f_n < 1. Therefore, 1/f_n > 10. The operating frequency is safe.
